      "text": "Dancehall is party music built for a crowd under speaker stacks: dancing, flexing, courtship, rivalry and celebration of the deejay’s personality. It grew out of Kingston’s sound systems, where a selector plays records, a deejay chats over them, and rival crews meet in sound clashes armed with exclusive dubplates. The audience is not passive. They shout, blow whistles and horns, flash lighters or phone lights, and demand a “pull up” (rewind) when a tune hits; a selector who reads them wrong gets called a soundboy. Listeners expect bass they feel in the chest, a kick that knocks, and every word of the patois lyric understood, because wordplay and quotable lines are the currency. The target is round bass, a chest-felt kick and crisp vocals that translate everywhere. The crowd will say “that sounded wrong” if the bass is boomy instead of round, the voice is buried or harsh, the riddim feels slow or soft, or a rewind lands late and kills the moment."
